From 20559ec99238055f13efdeeff122e3b9f8f5ee1d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: milliluk-erik <build@localhost>
Date: Mon, 3 Aug 2026 12:41:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 17/44] trap-actions: -trap-load (blob loader) +

The cycle counter is kept twice: xroar_cpu_cycles counts 6809 bus cycles,
xroar_master_cycles the same interval in master clock ticks (14.31818MHz),
summed from the tick count each bus cycle actually consumed. CPU cycles are
the right unit for almost everything and stay the default, but they are not
a time on this machine -- a cycle is 16 ticks at 0.89MHz and 8 at 1.79MHz,
so a count taken across a POKE 65497 converts to nothing at all. Master
ticks mean the same thing either side of a speed change.

-trap-cycle-master puts a master= field at the FRONT of the
-trap-cycle-print line and leaves cycles= in place: if you asked for master
ticks that is the number you came for, and anything matching on the field
name rather than its position keeps working either way. The ratio between
the two fields tells you what speed the machine was in.
-trap-cycle-reset zeroes both counters whether or not the flag is set, so
the two are always measuring the same interval.
